  "subtitle": {
    "en": "Red-necked and Grey Phalarope, Ruff, Knot, Purple Sandpiper, both snipes and the Avocet"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "The odd ones out. Phalaropes swim, snipes skulk, a Purple Sandpiper sits on a rock in the surf. Each of the eight birds in this lesson is a specialist, so each has a trick or a habit that gives it away.\n\nRed-necked Phalarope breeds on a few pools in the north of the UK, Ireland and Scandinavia, and passes in autumn on its way to the Arabian Sea. The Grey Phalarope is a seabird that you meet on land only after autumn gales. Ruff pass in huge numbers in spring and autumn, with the juveniles being the most distinctive. Knots gather in tens of thousands on a few estuaries in winter, and Purple Sandpipers keep to rocky coasts from October to April.\n\nSnipes and the Avocet finish the lesson. A Jack Snipe is a small, secretive winter bird that sits tight until you almost step on it."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "lobed toes"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "Toes with a flap of skin on each side, like a paddle. Phalaropes have them and swim."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "crown stripe"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A pale line along the middle of the crown. A Common Snipe has one; a Jack Snipe does not."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "scaly"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "With pale edges to each feather on the back, giving a scale-like pattern. Typical of juveniles."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "spinning"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The way a phalarope swims in tight circles to stir up food."
      }
    }
  ],
